{ Now let's change white for black and play the Dutch defense. }
1. d4 { The Dutch defense is robust at a Queen's pawn opening. } 1... f5 { [%csl Gb2] } 2. Nf3 { Not as common respons as g3. Trying to fianchetto the Bc1 bishop. } { [%cal Gb2b3,Bc1b2] } 2... e6 3. Nc3 { White is preparing an attack with the d-pawn. But it would be better to have it accompanied by the c-pawn. } { [%cal Bd4d5,Gc2c4] } 3... Nf6 { The black knight has multiple tasks. } { [%cal Gf6h5,Gf6d5,Bf6g4,Bf6e4] } 4. d5 Bb4 { Do not worry about the attack. Just develop and counter attack. If the e-pawn takes the white pawn, it gets isolated and can be grabbed later on. } 5. dxe6 d5 { No en passant and isolating the white pawn for later grabbing. } (5... dxe6 { The trick is not to recapture, }) 6. Qd3 O-O 7. a3 Bxc3+ 8. Qxc3 { Even at this moment we still not retake the pawn on e6 } 8... Ne4 9. e7 Qxe7 { Now was the forced moment to retake } 10. Qe5 Qd8 *


