{ Open with the bird }
1. f4 { [%clk 0:10:00] } 1... d5 { This is the normal variation. } { [%clk 0:10:00] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:09:59] } 2... Nc6 { [%clk 0:10:00] } 3. e3 { [%clk 0:09:57] } 3... e5 { Black sees the danger of Qh4 and tries to lure the protecting knight away. } { [%cal Gf4e5,Gf3e5,Gc6e5,Bd8h4] [%clk 0:10:00] } 4. fxe5 { [%clk 0:09:55] } 4... Nxe5 { [%clk 0:10:00] } 5. Nxe5 { [%clk 0:09:52] } 5... Qh4+ { Black want's you to move the g-pawn. Attacking both the rook and the knight. } { [%cal Gh4e4,Ge4h1,Gg2g3,Ge4e5] [%clk 0:09:57] } 6. g3 { [%clk 0:09:51] } 6... Qe4 { Although the threat seems dangerous, black is already losing. Offering his knight was fatal beceause he can not retrieve any compensation for it. } { [%clk 0:09:56] } 7. Nf3 { [%clk 0:09:47] } 7... Bg4 { Again black seems to launch a dangerous attack, but if white makes the right moves, there is no real danger. } { [%clk 0:09:55] } 8. Bg2 { [%clk 0:09:46] } 8... Nf6 { Black shoud have accepted his lost and castled. However, he tries to turn the tide by pressing his opponent. By this move he allows his Queen to be attacked and in doing so gets even more lagging behind in development. } { [%cal Ge8c8,Ga8d8] [%clk 0:09:47] } 9. O-O { Chasing the Queen away is the better approach. This is however the first move in a series that increasingly restricts the queen in her freedom of movement, trying to trap her in the end. } { [%cal Gf1f6] [%clk 0:09:45] } (9. Nc3 { This is the best move. As with d3 it chases away the Queen. Most likely to Qg6. Where white can ask for exchanging the Queens by moving his white knight. Another move is more popular though. Is uses the fact that there is a discovered attack on the black Queen. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gd2d3,Gf3e5,Gf3h4] }) 9... Bc5 { Blacks helps you by this move with finding the next move in trapping the black Queen! } { [%clk 0:09:43] } 10. d4 { [%clk 0:09:36] } 10... Bb6 { The Queen has only four squares left that can be removed in one move by white, White hopes that black is not aware of this because of the many options the Queen still has, because white needs a Zwischezug (in between move) before he can play this out. } { [%cal Re4d4,Re4g4,Re4c2,Re4f3,Re4e5,Ge4e6,Ge4e7,Ge4f5,Ge4g6] [%clk 0:09:39] } 11. Qd2 { [%clk 0:09:21] } 11... O-O { Now trap the Queen. } { [%clk 0:09:36] } 12. Ne5 { [%clk 0:09:15] } 12... Ba5 { [%clk 0:09:16] } 13. Qxa5 (13. c3 { This is ok, but you can even take more advantage. The Queen can be also trapped on Qc2. } { [%csl Rc2] [%clk 0:09:12] }) 13... Qxc2 { Now trap the Queen for the second time! } 14. Nc3 { Now you only have to collect the Queen by Rf2. } { [%cal Rc2b3,Rc2a4,Rc2d3,Rc2e4,Rc2f5,Rc2g6,Rc2d2,Rc2e2,Rc2f2,Rc2b1,Rc2d1,Gf1f2] } 14... Be2 { This will only provides white with another Bishop. } 15. Rf2 b6 { No help there for black, but be careful where you put your Queen. } 16. Qa3 Ne4 { Also no help there. } 17. Rxe2 *


