{ The Queen can be moved in early by black without losing the game. }
1. f4 d5 2. Nf3 Qd6 { The black Queen attacks the pawn on f4 and has plans to move his h-pawn and exchange it so the h-file opens. His rook and Queen can double up for a fierce attack.
If you like gambits then you will enjoy yourself now because the best move for white is to sacrifice a pawn and ignoring the threat on his f-pawn. } { [%cal Gd6f4,Bd6h6,Bh7h5] } 3. c4 dxc4 { Now attack the pawn that just captured yours. In this attacking play you dominate black. } 4. e3 b5 5. a4 c6 { Black continues to defend the pawn in vain. } 6. Na3 Bd7 { Black wants to keep the pawn lead at all costs! However, he gives away his position, so that in reality white is well ahead. } 7. axb5 cxb5 { Up to now the fact that the black Queen is exposed early in the game could not be used by white. } 8. b3 cxb3 9. Nxb5 Qb6 { Now black loses his pawn anyway because moving the Queen to Qd5 means a fork by the white knight. } { [%cal Gd6d5,Gb5c7,Gc7a8,Gc7d5] } (9... Qd5) (9... Bxb5 10. Bxb5+) 10. Qxb3 *


