{ One of the advantages of moving the f-pawn two places is that you already build in the possibility of an early rook lift in the middle game. }
1. f4 Nc6 { With the Nimzowitsch Defence black protects the e5 square. } 2. Nf3 { This move is the most common one in the f-pawn openings. Bringing in the knight on the f-file. } 2... d6 { Black doubles it's defense on e4 after the white also doubles it's attack. } 3. e4 { White takes the space offered by black. } 3... e5 { Black takes the opportunity in turn to claim space in the middle and attack d4 and f4. } 4. d3 Bg4 { Also a very common situation in the Bird where black uses the space on the g-file to pin the knight. } 5. Be2 Nf6 { This frequently occurring arrangement offers the possibility of preparing a future rook lift. } 6. O-O { The rook lift is prepared, activating the otherwise passive rook early in the game. Rooks are powerful pieces for an attack on the opponent's king. } { [%cal Gf1f3,Gf3g3,Gf3h3] } *


