1. f4 { With the Birdopening white tries to attack the e5 square, with the intention to eventually place his knight on Ne5. One way to prevent this is by the From gambit. } 1... e5 (1... g5 { To attack with another pawn is the correct idea. However this pawn is captured without getting it back soon. White can simply defend this pawn by d4 when black moves it's e-pawn to attack the g5 pawn. }) 2. fxe5 { The best move for white is to accept the gambit. If white however refuses by playing e4, then the position transposes into the King's gambit. White can safely take the pawn because Qh4 followed by Qe4 can be met with g3 and Nf3. } { [%cal Rd8h4,Rh4e4,Gg1f3,Re4h1,Gg2g3] } 2... d6 { This is the main line of the From's Gambit. } (2... Bc5 { With this move black tries to make use of the weak h4-e1 diagonal of white. When white would block the bishop by d4, black can give a check and collect this pawn. However white can use his knight to protect h4 first.
Bringing out the knights on the g-file is often one of the first moves black or white will play in the f-pawn openings to protect the weak diagonal. } { [%csl Rd8,Bg3,Bf2,Be1,Rh4,Re5,Rd4][%cal Rd8h4,Gg1f3,Rh4d4,Rd2d4] }) (2... Nc6 { This seems at first a logic move to attack the pawn and to develop a piece at the same time. This is called the Schlechter gambit. However white can easily defend this pawn and the h4-e1 diagonal by moving his knight.
Bringing out the knights on the g-file is often one of the first moves black or white will play in the f-pawn openings to protect the weak diagonal. } { [%csl Rh4,Bg3,Bf2,Be1,Re5][%cal Gg1f3,Rd8h4,Rc6e5] }) (2... Qg5 { This is not the best way to get back the pawn. The white knight can easily attack the Queen and defend the pawn at the same time. Furthermore, the knight protects the h4 square.
Bringing out the knights on the g-file is often one of the first moves black or white will play in the f-pawn openings to protect the weak diagonal. } { [%csl Gf3,Re5,Rh4,Bg3,Bf2,Be1,Rg5][%cal Gg1f3,Gf3g5] }) (2... Qh4+ { This is a natural but bad move. The weak diagonal that the Queen uses, is in fact not weak at all yet. This check gives white the opportunity to attack the Queen while strengthen his position. The pawn offer will not lead to an advantage for black.
Bringing out the knights on the g-file is often one of the first moves black or white will play in the f-pawn openings to protect the weak diagonal. In this situation, that move can be postponed because the pawn already offers enough protection } { [%csl Rg1][%cal Gg2g3,Bd2d4,Rg1f3] }) 3. Nf3 { You would expect that white would immediately cash in the second pawn sacrifice. This is called the Lasker Variaton. This is the best option. Because black can recapture with Bd6 and then chase away the knight on Nf3 to put pressure on the king side after Qh4+, this is hard to play for white. } { [%cal Re5d6,Rf8d6,Rc8g4,Rd8h4] } (3. exd6 { This is called the Lasker Variaton. This seems to be the best option according to the computer (depth 43). Because black can recapture with Bd6 and then chase away the knight on Nf3 to put pressure on the king side after Qh4+ this is hard for humans to play, so we advise Nf3 first. } { [%cal Rf8d6,Gg1f3,Rc8g4,Rd8h4] }) 3... dxe5 { This is a correct move. However consider another move-order for the more fancy solution. } (3... Nc6 { Congratulations. This move-order is the best solution. It will lead to the same position when capturing the pawn immediately. }) 4. Nxe5 Bd6 { This is the main-line. } (4... Qh4+ { This attack looks dangerous. But when white plays correctly after g3 with his knight, it is not and white gains a strong advantage. } { [%cal Gg2g3,Bh4e4,Be5f3,Yh4h5,Ye5d3] }) 5. Nf3 { You can train the From Gambit on Lichess Study https://lichess.org/study/oPzrFRwh. The lines there are stockfish recommendations for white (level 37 or higher) applied to common moves by black. To train the lines in that study with tempo, download the study pgn and upload it into https://chesstempo.com/ for example. } 5... Bg4 6. e3 { When the knight on Nf3 falls the threat Qh4+ means losing the rook on Rh1. Create space for the King and at the same time bring in another defender on the king side when the kngith falls. Also the trick for black to sacrifice his bishop Bxg3 to capture the rook is nog working. } { [%cal Rd8h4,Rg4f3,Gd1f3,Gg2g3,Bd6g3,Bf3g3] } *


