{ Open with the Bird. }
1. f4 e5 2. fxe5 Nc6 { This is the Schlechter gambit } 3. Nf3 (3. Nc3 { This is a fine move. Let's continu however with another slightly better one that defends both the pawn and the black Queen from checking! } { [%csl Ge5,Gh4] }) 3... Bc5 { The a7-g1 diagonal is often a challenging danger for White in the Bird opening with the f-pawn gone. } 4. e3 { It is indeed wise to build a strong defense as soon as possible protecting the vulnerable diagonal. } (4. d4 { Do not immediately chase the bishop away because the pawn on d4 is not really defended by the knight on Nf3 after the black Queen delivers a check on Qh4. Either valid respons by white means losing the knight. } { [%cal Gc6d4,Rf3d4,Gd8h4,Gh4d4,Bg2g3,Be1d2] }) (4. c3 { A good move. Preparing d4. There is a quicker defense though that also builds a double pawn blokkade when followed by d4 on the a7-g1 diagonal. } { [%cal Gd2d4] }) *


