1. e4 { In the From Gambit chapter you saw how black could immediately throw a gambit if white opens with the f-pawn. This also happens if black opens with the f-pawn after white start with e4. } 1... f5 { This is the Duras Gambit. If you don not want to offer the f-pawn but still wants to use it as the opening pawn, consider the Barnes defense (f6). } { [%cal Gf7f6] } (1... d5 { This is the Scandinavian Defence but not a gambit because the Queen protects the d5 pawn. }) (1... f6 { This is the Barnes defense. It is a f-pawn defense alternative for the Duras hambit which avoids offering the f-pawn immediately. }) 2. exf5 { The best reponse for white is to accept the gambit. } 2... Nf6 { This is the move black should play to prevent losing. } { [%cal Rd1h5] } (2... Nh6 { This is not as good as Nf6 but protects } { [%cal Gg8f6,Rd1h5,Rh5e8] } 3. Qh5+ Nf7) 3. Be2 { This line is a dangerous one for black because white threatens on h5 to deliver check. } 3... d6 { This seems the best way to protect black against a devastating King side attack. } (3... g6 { Although this holds the attack on the King side, in total white has after it captures the pawn on g6 gained a pawn from black and has destroyed the king side structure, This option is however not much weaker than another approach. }) 4. Bh5+ Nxh5 (4... Kd7 { Give a Zwischenzug before using the escape route to prevent the white bishop to go to f7. }) (4... g6 { This will not work. After the white pawn takes on g7 the same threats still exists but with no white pawn to block checking the king on Kd7. }) 5. Qxh5+ Kd7 { The white pawn prevents his own Queen for delivering another check. } (5... g6 { This is a mistake because the white pawn can take and if you take back you lose the rook. }) 6. Qg4 { This is actually not the strongest move. That is Nf3. But black has to be careful about the discovered attack when the white pawn moves. There are two ways to handle this correctly. Choose the way that does not lead to a repetition of moves. } 6... h5 7. Qa4+ c6 *


