[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/nnpw9W8v/izhPxN6N"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Bijbrengen"]
[UTCDate "2022.07.19"]
[UTCTime "17:29:40"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

1. e4 f5 2. e5 d6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bb5 dxe5 5. Nxe5 Qd5 6. Bxc6+ bxc6 7. Nf3 g5 { This is the best defense for white. Why you will notice when you choose the best move after white continues another way. } { [%cal Gb1c3] } 8. d4 { If white would have captured the pawn, the exchange will equalize the position. } { [%cal Rf3g5,Gd5g2] } 8... Qe4+ { This is the reason for the earlier mentioned knight move defending this square. } { [%cal Bb1c3] } 9. Be3 f4 { Although the Queen move was played in a game as a reaction to black's pawn move, this effectively means losing the black bishop. This can be countered though! } { [%csl Rd1][%cal Rd1d3] } 10. Nc3 { Again chasing away the Queen which can not occupy any square on the e-file maintaining the pin. } { [%cal Re4e5,Re4e6,Gf3g5,Gf3e5] } 10... Qf5 11. Ne5 { This looks surprising. One would expect white to whitdraw the bishop, which is fine but not the best move because it keeps on the pressure by playing the black bishop to Ba6. } { [%cal Re3d2,Gc8a6] } 11... Bg7 { You noticed the danger that always exists in the Dutch defense, the check by Qh5. You can not prevent this without sacrifizing the Queen. So you attack the second attacker instead, the knight on Ne5. } { [%cal Gd1h5,Rf5g6,Rf5f7] } (11... fxe3 { This obvious move could cost you a rook and a pawn (if the white knight catches the pawn on c6 in between) because the Queen can put a check freeing the f7 square. The black King has to move first to Kd8. Then it is forced to d7 when the knight goes to Nc6. When the knight moves back the King could go to Ke6. But then white will castle and open the f-file. This will cost black the Queen which is trapped. This can be only defended by offering the pawn on e3. Even with strong counter play by black it will at least lose a pawn and crashes it's position. } { [%cal Ge8d8,Gd8d7,Gd7e8,Gd1h5,Ge5c6,Gc6e5,Ge5f7,Bd7e6,Be1g1,Bf2e3,Ge3e2] }) 12. Qf3 { You could not prevent white from checking you, but the bishop move has another advantage, it clears the escape square f7. Checking has no use because after you simply move youre king the dangerous f7 square keeps being defended twice. } { [%cal Rf3h5,Ge8f8,Bf5f7,Bf8f7] } *


