[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Bijbrengen"]
[UTCDate "2022.07.10"]
[UTCTime "07:42:48"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

1. e4 { Attack the e-pawn by your f-pawn } 1... f5 2. exf5 { White accepts the Duras gambit } 2... Nf6 { This is the best and most common move in the f-pawn openings. To bring the knight to the f-file to prevent mating patterns by white on the h-file. } 3. d3 { White wants to prevent a outpost for the black knight. This is not the best move by white because the knight can not move because of the King's safety anyway. Better is d4 which white will correct later. Watch out for white removing your important knight. } 3... d5 { There is no mating threat because the King can escape. Black wants to control the center now the white e-pawn is out of place. Besides that, it tries to win back this pawn by the bishop. If white allows that, the King side danger is limited after Bxf5. } { [%cal Gc8f5,Gf5g6] } 4. Bg5 { Black still wants to remove the black knight so that both players no longer have an e-pawn. } 4... Bxf5 5. d4 { White now sees that it was better to move the pawn to d4 immediately. } 5... Ne4 { This outpost for the knight is what you often encounter after f-pawn openings because the e4 (black) or the e5 (white) is mostly free after the f-pawn move. } *


