{ When the opponent starts with e-pawn, the f-pawn-opening transposes into a "weak" gambit (From when playing white, and Duras when playing black). This is obvious not the case for a Queen pawn opening by the opponent. This is the reason that f-pawn openings are most common in combination with a Queen pawn opening. }
1. f4 d5 { This is the Dutch Variation of the Bird opening because you can handle the position as a Dutch Defense reversed. } 2. Nf3 Nc6 { Black threatens to move his queen pawn to disrupt your pawn build-up. } { [%cal Gd5d4] } 3. e3 { If black now pushes it's queen pawn do not take it, but bring out your bishop.
If black takes your e3 pawn, do not recapture it (to prevent Queen exchange and loosing castling rights), bit push your d-pawn forward. You can capture the black pawn any moment you want to because it is attacked and can not be defended.
An even fancier approach is to bring the knight to Ne5 instead of pushing the pawn, but this requires precise play. } *


