[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

1. Nf3 (1. f4) 1... f5 2. g3 { White wants to fianchetto the bishop on Bg2. } { [%cal Gf1g2,Bg2a8] } 2... Nf6 (2... g6 { This is indeed the best respons. But let's try another approach, by using the standard reaction with the horse. }) 3. Bg2 { The white bishop finishes off the fianchetto aim. Black could aim for the same approach putting his bishop on g7. However let's try another option first, forcing the white pawn on the d4 square or getting control over 4th row on the king side The last position is a chararestic of the Dutch opening. } { [%cal Gd2d4] } 3... d6 { The best move for white is d4. } { [%cal Ge7e5] } (3... g6 { This is the best move, but let's try to get tease white to move the d-pawn! }) 4. O-O { A common move by white. } 4... e5 5. c4 { With this move white wants to create a attack diagonal preventing castling or to set up a discovered attack. Prepare a quick attack on the white king. This is one of the advantages of the Dutch defense. } { [%cal Gd1b3,Bb3g8,Bc4c5] } 5... Be7 6. d3 a5 { White wanted to advance on the Queen side. } { [%cal Gb2b4] } 7. a3 { White still wants to attack on the Queen side by protecting b4. } { [%cal Ga3b4] } 7... O-O { We have time though because white can not move d4 because it will lose the rook after the pawn exchange } { [%cal Ra8a1,Rb2b4,Ra3b4,Ga5b4] } 8. Nc3 { The black Queen wants to fly out to h5 for the attack. However black can protect itself against this attack by moving his pieces to the king side too. } { [%cal Gc1g5] } 8... Qe8 (8... h6 { This move is fine bu there is a better and more attacking move! }) 9. Rb1 { White wants move d4 to now, ignoring the threat on his king side. Look at the lines available to black for attacking. You should protect your attack first. There are two good ways. Which is the best one? } { [%cal Ge8h5,Gf5f4,Ge7h4,Gf6g4,Gb8d7,Bd7f6,Bc8h3,Rb2b4] } 9... h6 { This is the best one, preventing the bishop on Bc1 on the knight on Nf3 to defend. } { [%cal Rc1g5,Rf3g5] } (9... Kh8 { This is a good move anticipating a attack by the white Queen on the a2-g8 diagonal. However this threat takes two moves for white. } { [%cal Gd1b3,Bc4c5] }) 10. b4 axb4 11. axb4 { You can continue practising the Zukertort opening defending by the Dutch on https://lichess.org/study/0tmQQdBm } *


