1. d4 f5 2. h3 Nf6 3. g4 d5 (3... fxg4 { The danger black has to face comes from the open file for the rook. By offering the rook white can checkmate on g6 if black is not careful. } { [%csl Rd3][%cal Rh3g4,Rh1h7,Gf6g4,Rd1d3,Rd3g6] } 4. hxg4 Nxg4 5. Qd3 g6 6. Rxh7 Rxh7 7. Qxg6+ Rf7 8. Qxg4) 4. gxf5 Nc6 (4... Bxf5 { Black has the advantage if it's bishop captures the white pawn on f5. However, we can wait for this move! }) 5. Qd3 Ne4 { This is why we could wait before capturing the pawn on f5 by the bishop. } 6. f3 Ng3 (6... Bxf5 { This also works fine. But there is even a stronger move! } { [%cal Rf3e4,Gf5e4,Ge4h1,Ge4d3] }) 7. Rh2 Bxf5 (7... Nxf5 { The black knight is safe for now. So use this move to develop and attack. }) 8. Qc3 e5 9. dxe5 Bb4 *


